  <section class="bio-section">
    <h2>What is MooMore, briefly?</h2>

    <div class="bio-card featured">
      <p>
        MooMore uses thermal imaging and on-device AI to detect inflammation in dairy cows before lameness becomes visible.
        It won the Innovate UK Women in Innovation Award 2025/26, announced on 5 August 2026, with a £75,000 grant and a
        twelve-month programme running to June 2027.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is currently in validation on a 650-cow crossbred herd in Staffordshire.
        <a href="https://moomore.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full project details at moomore.com</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bio-section">
    <h2>Why move from crops to livestock?</h2>

    <div class="bio-card">
      <p>
        The problem I work on has not changed. It is the same one it has always been: a measurement that matters is
        available only to people with specialist equipment or expert judgement, so most of the people who need it never
        take it.
      </p>

      <p>
        With Petiole Pro that measurement was leaf area, and the answer was a smartphone camera. With MooMore it is
        inflammation, and the answer is a thermal sensor. The crop and the cow are different; the shape of the problem
        is identical.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bio-section">
    <h2>What took four years?</h2>

    <div class="bio-card">
      <p>
        Two things, and neither of them was the science. The first was finding a sensor configuration that holds up in a
        working milking parlour rather than a laboratory — warm, wet, busy, and entirely uninterested in what worked on
        the bench. The second, and the harder one, was finding a farm willing to let me try.
      </p>

      <p>
        That second one is the reason so much good agritech never leaves the demo stage. You can have a promising signal
        and still have nowhere real to test it. What you need is someone who farms for a living, who has every reason to
        say no, and who says yes anyway.
      </p>

      <p>
        So I want to say a proper thank you to <strong>William Sanders</strong>, Farm Manager at
        <strong>Major Farming Ltd</strong>, who opened his parlour and his herd to this project. William and I are both
        Oxford Farming Conference 2026 Inspire Programme Fellows — we met through Inspire, and this trial exists because
        of that. He has given the project his time, his judgement, and his patience with a camera in the parlour, and
        MooMore is a far better idea for it. Thank you, William. I could not have done this without you.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “It took four years to find the right farm partner and technology, but I'm determined to prove women can lead the
        future of agritech.”
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bio-section">
    <h2>What does the Innovate UK award change?</h2>

    <div class="bio-card">
      <p>
        It funds twelve months of validation with a named partner, which is the step that turns a plausible signal into
        evidence. The findings will be published whether or not the signal holds — that was the commitment when the
        project started and it has not changed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The award was made in the programme's tenth-anniversary round: 100 women recognised from 1,590 applications, of
        whom 61 received grants.
      </p>

